  "articleBody": "Homeowners needing extra living area often weigh converting an attached garage against framing a brand-new ground-up home addition. Both approaches expand your home, but the structural paths differ significantly.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options\nConverting a garage takes advantage of existing exterior framing, footings, and a roof, which generally leads to a faster turnaround and lower framing costs. However, garages feature uninsulated concrete slabs that sit lower than the main house floor, often requiring step adjustments, vapor barriers, and full thermal retrofits. You also give up enclosed vehicle parking and storage, which may trigger local zoning rules regarding replacement parking.\n\nA ground-up addition leaves your parking intact and allows you to design your floor plan completely from scratch without working around existing slab heights. On the other hand, additions involve excavation, new foundation pours, roof tie-ins, and longer project timelines with greater exposure to weather delays.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nYour final decision often hinges on property line setbacks, electrical service capacity, and how easily plumbing drainage can tie into your primary sewer lines if you are adding a bathroom or kitchenette. When you are ready to evaluate your space and plan the remodel, contact Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
